    "terminal_reason": "completed",
    "result_text": "I fixed `mock_openai()` in `tests/fakes.py`, and both digest tests pass now.\n\n**Cause:** openai 3.19.2 no longer sends requests through `httpx`. It uses `httpx2`, a separate package with its own `httpcore2` underneath. respx only patches `httpx` and `httpcore`, so the mock never saw these requests and they went to the real network.\n\n**Fix:** the helper keeps its interface and its respx router. Inside the `with` block it now also patches `httpx2.Client._transport_for_url`, which is the same hook respx uses for `httpx`. The patch sends every request from an `httpx2` client through the router. So the canned replies, the `seen` list and the 400 once replies run out all work as before. Because it's patched on the class, it covers app.digest's cached client and clients created before or inside the block. The patch is removed when the block exits. The existing respx mock still covers OpenAI clients given a plain `httpx.Client`.\n\n**Checks:**\n- `tests/test_digest.py`: 2 passed.\n- Scratch test for the other docstring promises:\n  - A client created before the block got the canned reply.\n  - A client created inside the block got a `BadRequestError` once replies ran out.\n  - `seen` recorded both requests.\n  - After the block, a request went to the real network and pytest-socket blocked it. That shows nothing is intercepted afterwards. The scratch test's final assertion failed only because I expected the wrong error type.\n\n**One thing for you to do:** my scratch file `tests/test_zz_check.py` is still there because deleting it needed approval. Please delete it, since it fails because of that wrong expected error type. Once it's gone, `pytest -q` passes.\n\nThe \"nothing warns about it\" part isn't fixed in general. A future change like this would still fail silently in the mock. It would only show up as a blocked-network error in the tests, like this one did."
  },
